`usb_get_dev()` increments the USB device reference count. However, when subsequent allocations or operations in `gpio_mpsse_probe()` fail (e.g., `ida_alloc()` error leading to `return priv->id;`), the function returns without calling `usb_put_dev()`. This causes a reference leak because the count is never decremented on these error paths.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
<text>
static void gpio_mpsse_usb_put_dev(void *data)
{
	struct mpsse_priv *priv = data;
	usb_put_dev(priv->udev);
}
</text>
<text>
err = devm_add_action_or_reset(dev, gpio_mpsse_usb_put_dev, priv);
if (err)
	return err;
</text>
Reason:
The `devm_add_action_or_reset()` registers a device-managed action that calls `gpio_mpsse_usb_put_dev()` automatically when the device is unbound or if the probe fails. This ensures the reference obtained by `usb_get_dev()` is always released, even on error paths. The manual `usb_put_dev()` call in the disconnect function is removed (shown in patch as `-usb_put_dev(priv->udev);`) to avoid a double release, since the managed action now handles cleanup on disconnect as well.

